---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import Section from "../../components/Section.astro";
import Container from "../../components/Container.astro";
import Prose from "../../components/Prose.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";
import Lightbox from "../../components/Lightbox.vue";
import placeholderRaster from "../../assets/placeholder-raster.jpg";
import placeholderSvg from "../../assets/placeholder.svg";
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Enterprise Design System"
  eyebrow="TechCorp Global"
  standfirst="Building a scalable design system that unified 15 product teams and reduced design-to-development handoff time by 60%."
  byline="Principal Designer · 2024–2025"
  tags={["Design Systems", "Component Libraries", "Documentation"]}
>
  <!-- Challenge -->
  <Section>
    <Container>
      <Prose>
        <h2>The Challenge</h2>
        <p>
          TechCorp Global's product ecosystem had grown to 15 distinct
          applications serving millions of users across web, iOS, and Android
          platforms. Each team maintained its own component libraries,
          resulting in:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Inconsistent user experiences across products, confusing customers
          </li>
          <li>
            Duplicated effort — teams rebuilding the same components repeatedly
          </li>
          <li>
            Long design-to-development cycles, averaging 6–8 weeks per feature
          </li>
          <li>
            Accessibility gaps, with WCAG 2.1 AA compliance varying widely
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Leadership recognized that a unified design system was essential to
          scale product development while maintaining quality and consistency.
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <!-- Approach -->
  <Section>
    <Container size="wide">
      <Figure
        src={placeholderRaster}
        alt="Design system component library overview showing buttons, inputs, and cards"
        caption="Component library snapshot: 120+ production-ready components built with accessibility and theming built in"
        credit="Screenshot from internal design system documentation"
      />
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section>
    <Container>
      <Prose>
        <h2>Approach</h2>
        <h3>Research & Audit</h3>
        <p>
          I led a comprehensive audit of all 15 product UIs, cataloging every
          unique component variant, pattern, and interaction. This revealed 37
          distinct button implementations alone — clearly unsustainable.
        </p>
        <p>
          Through stakeholder interviews with designers, engineers, and product
          managers, I identified the core pain points:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Engineers wanted plug-and-play components, not design specs</li>
          <li>
            Designers needed flexible primitives that could compose into
            product-specific layouts
          </li>
          <li>
            Product teams required fast iteration without sacrificing quality
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Foundation: Design Tokens</h3>
        <p>
          Rather than jumping straight to components, I established a
          token-driven foundation using the Material Design 3 spec as a
          baseline, customized for TechCorp's brand:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Color system:</strong> Full MD3 schemes supporting light/dark
            modes plus medium and high contrast variants (912 tokens total, including
            state layers with preserved alpha channels)
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Typography:</strong> Custom editorial and UI scales mapped to
            brand fonts (Platypi for headlines, Instrument Sans for body, IBM Plex
            Mono for code)
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Spacing & shape:</strong> Semantic editorial spacing and corner
            radius tokens
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          All tokens were built with <a href="https://amzn.github.io/style-dictionary/"
            >Style Dictionary</a
          > and exported to CSS custom properties, iOS Swift constants, and
          Android XML resources — enabling true cross-platform consistency.
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <!-- Gallery -->
  <Section>
    <Container>
      <Prose>
        <h3>Component Library</h3>
        <p>
          With the foundation in place, I designed and engineered 120+
          production components across three tiers:
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section>
    <Container size="wide">
      <div class="gallery-grid">
        <img src={placeholderSvg.src} alt="Component example 1" />
        <img src={placeholderSvg.src} alt="Component example 2" />
        <img src={placeholderSvg.src} alt="Component example 3" />
      </div>
      <Lightbox
        client:idle
        images={[
          {
            src: placeholderRaster.src,
            alt: "Design system component tier 1: primitives",
            caption: "Tier 1: Atomic primitives (buttons, inputs, badges)",
          },
          {
            src: placeholderRaster.src,
            alt: "Design system component tier 2: patterns",
            caption: "Tier 2: Compositional patterns (cards, forms, navigation)",
          },
          {
            src: placeholderRaster.src,
            alt: "Design system component tier 3: templates",
            caption:
              "Tier 3: Page templates (dashboards, settings, onboarding)",
          },
        ]}
      />
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section>
    <Container>
      <Prose>
        <p>
          Each component was built in Figma with matching code implementations
          in React (web), SwiftUI (iOS), and Jetpack Compose (Android). Code
          Connect files linked design to code, enabling engineers to copy
          production code directly from Figma.
        </p>

        <h3>Documentation & Adoption</h3>
        <p>
          A design system is only as good as its adoption. I built a dedicated
          documentation site with:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Live component playground with editable props</li>
          <li>Accessibility guidelines for each component</li>
          <li>Design rationale and usage patterns</li>
          <li>Migration guides for legacy code</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          I also ran weekly office hours and onboarding sessions, training 200+
          designers and engineers on the system.
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <!-- Results -->
  <Section>
    <Container>
      <Prose>
        <h2>Results</h2>
        <p>
          After 18 months of development and rollout, the design system
          transformed how TechCorp ships product:
        </p>
      </Prose>
      <div class="metrics-grid">
        <div class="metric-card">
          <p class="metric-value">60%</p>
          <p class="metric-label">Faster handoff</p>
        </div>
        <div class="metric-card">
          <p class="metric-value">15</p>
          <p class="metric-label">Teams unified</p>
        </div>
        <div class="metric-card">
          <p class="metric-value">120+</p>
          <p class="metric-label">Components shipped</p>
        </div>
        <div class="metric-card">
          <p class="metric-value">100%</p>
          <p class="metric-label">WCAG 2.1 AA</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <Prose>
        <p>
          But the qualitative impact was even more significant: designers and
          engineers finally spoke the same language. Product teams could now
          focus on solving user problems instead of rebuilding buttons.
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            "Before the design system, we spent weeks recreating basic UI. Now
            we prototype in hours and ship in days. It's transformed how we
            work."
          </p>
          <p class="attribution">
            — Sarah Chen, Senior Product Designer, TechCorp Global
          </p>
        </blockquote>
      </Prose>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <!-- Reflection -->
  <Section>
    <Container>
      <Prose>
        <h2>Reflection</h2>
        <p>
          This project reinforced a key lesson: design systems succeed when
          they're built <em>with</em> teams, not <em>for</em> them. The audit,
          stakeholder interviews, and iterative rollout ensured the system solved
          real problems rather than imposing top-down abstractions.
        </p>
        <p>
          If I were to do it again, I'd invest even more in automation —
          specifically, automated visual regression testing and token validation
          to catch drift earlier. I'd also push harder for executive sponsorship
          to secure dedicated maintenance resources; a design system is never "done."
        </p>
        <p>
          The system continues to evolve today, with 6 core maintainers and
          contributions from dozens of team members across the company.
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </Container>
  </Section>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  .gallery-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr));
    gap: var(--md-sys-spacing-body-to-body);
    margin-bottom: var(--md-sys-spacing-body-to-body);
  }

  .gallery-grid img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: var(--md-sys-shape-corner-medium);
    border: 1px solid var(--md-sys-color-outline-variant);
  }

  .metrics-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr));
    gap: var(--md-sys-spacing-body-to-body);
    margin: var(--md-sys-spacing-body-to-subsection) 0;
  }

  .metric-card {
    padding: var(--md-sys-spacing-body-to-subsection);
    background-color: var(--md-sys-color-surface-container);
    border-radius: var(--md-sys-shape-corner-large);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .metric-value {
    font-family: var(--md-sys-typescale-metric-font);
    font-size: var(--md-sys-typescale-metric-size);
    font-weight: var(--md-sys-typescale-metric-weight);
    line-height: var(--md-sys-typescale-metric-line-height);
    letter-spacing: var(--md-sys-typescale-metric-tracking);
    color: var(--md-sys-color-primary);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .metric-label {
    font-family: var(--md-sys-typescale-label-medium-font);
    font-size: var(--md-sys-typescale-label-medium-size);
    font-weight: var(--md-sys-typescale-label-medium-weight);
    line-height: var(--md-sys-typescale-label-medium-line-height);
    letter-spacing: var(--md-sys-typescale-label-medium-tracking);
    color: var(--md-sys-color-on-surface-variant);
    margin: var(--md-sys-spacing-eyebrow-to-title) 0 0 0;
  }

  .attribution {
    font-family: var(--md-sys-typescale-attribution-font);
    font-size: var(--md-sys-typescale-attribution-size);
    font-weight: var(--md-sys-typescale-attribution-weight);
    line-height: var(--md-sys-typescale-attribution-line-height);
    letter-spacing: var(--md-sys-typescale-attribution-tracking);
    color: var(--md-sys-color-on-surface-variant);
  }
</style>
